<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.17.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01176</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.17.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6e2d1af4-e630-46ed-b201-863c0fdcdc17</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="4346f584-c163-4d4f-af5b-ff3f4e32e6dc" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For questions 1 – 3, match each sequence with one of the definitions. Note that only the part of the definition showing the relationship between the current term and the previous term is given so as not to give away the solutions.</para><para>6, 12, 18, 24</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a(n)=7\cdot a(n-1)\)</para><para>\(b(n)=\frac12b(n-1)\)</para><para>\(c(n)=c(n-1)+6\)</para><para>\(d(n)=d(n-1)+20\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(c(n)=c(n-1)+6\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.17.2. </para><para>The correct answer is \(c(n)=c(n−1)+6\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="f0bb4279-bc18-410e-9f1e-cf2a4f7c4a18" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>2, 14, 98, 686</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a{(\mathrm n})=7\cdot a(n-1)\)</para><para>\(b(n)=\frac12b(n-1)\)</para><para>\(c(n)=c(n-1)+6\)</para><para>\(d(n)=d(n-1)+20\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a(n)=7\cdot a(n-1)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.17.2.</para><para>The correct answer is \(a(n))=7\cdot a(n-1)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="9e8f3213-c781-466f-b0c9-960969c82d9e" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>160, 80, 40, 20</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a{(\mathrm n})=7\cdot a(n-1)\)</para><para>\(b(n)=\frac12b(n-1)\)</para><para>\(c(n)=c(n-1)+6\)</para><para>\(d(n)=d(n-1)+20\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(b(n)=\frac{1}{2}b(n-1)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.17.2. </para><para>The correct answer is \(b(n)=\frac12b(n-1)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="3a1ca587-2e2c-4c2b-9366-97eff10d778b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For questions 4 – 5, select the first five terms of each sequence. Determine whether each sequence is arithmetic, geometric, or neither.</para><para>\(a(1)=7\), \(a(n)=a(n−1)−3\) for \(n≥2\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>7, 4, 1, -2, -5, geometric</para><para>7, -21, 63, -189, geometric</para><para>7, -21, 63, -189, arithmetic</para><para>7, 4, 1, -2, -5, arithmetic</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 7, 4, 1, –2, –5, arithmetic</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.17.2.</para><para>The correct answer is 7, 4, 1, –2, –5, arithmetic. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="a3ae435e-517b-4dd8-a419-01290f5af8d7" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(c(1)=3\), \(c(n)=10⋅c(n−1)\) for \(n≥2\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>3, 30, 300, 3000, 30000, arithmetic</para><para>3, 30, 300, 3000, 30000, geometric</para><para>3, 13, 23, 33, 43, geometric</para><para>3, 13, 23, 33, 43, arithmetic</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! 3, 30, 300, 3000, 30000, geometric</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 4.17.2. </para><para>The correct answer is 3, 30, 300, 3000, 30000, geometric. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="50bf737d-9ce3-4460-aee2-daf2767eecf9" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>For questions 6 – 8, match each sequence with one of the recursive definitions. Note that only the part of the definition showing the relationship between the current term and the previous term is given so as not to give away the solutions.</para><para>3, 15, 75, 375</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a(n)=\frac{1}{3} \cdot a(n-1)\)</para><para>\(b(n)=b(n-1)-4\)</para><para>\(c(n)=5 \cdot c(n-1)\)</para><para>\(d(n)=d(n-1)+n-1\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(c(n)=5 \cdot c(n-1)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.17.2 – 4.17.3.</para><para>The correct answer is \(c(n)=5\cdot c(n−1)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="de90b110-dd9b-4dab-a220-43138f2ab7ac" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>18, 6, 2, \(\frac{2}{3}\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a(n)=\frac{1}{3}\cdot a(n-1)\)</para><para>\(b(n)=b(n-1)-4\)</para><para>\(c(n)=5 \cdot c(n-1)\)</para><para>\(d(n)=d(n-1)+n-1\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(a(n)=\frac{1}{3}a(n-1)\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.17.2 – 4.17.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(a(n)=\frac{1}{3}\cdot a(n-1)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="cbd7a717-07d5-4ad6-a639-e457a6d9956c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>17, 13, 9, 5</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(a(n)=\frac{1}{3}\cdot a(n-1)\)</para><para>\(b(n)=b(n-1)-4\)</para><para>\(c(n)=5 \cdot c(n-1)\)</para><para>\(d(n)=d(n-1)+n-1\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(b(n)=b(n-1)-4\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.17.2-–.16.3. </para><para>The correct answer is \(b(n)=b(n-1)-4\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="70384a16-aac0-49c2-a57c-91d05f23be77" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para> An arithmetic sequence \(k\) starts 12, 6, . . . and is represented by the graph below. </para>
      <para>Which of the following is a recursive definition for sequence \(k\) when \(n\geq2\)?</para>
     
    <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n)-6\)</para><para>\(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n-1)-6\)</para><para>\(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n-1)+6\)</para><para>\(k(1)=12, 6⋅k(n)=k(n-1)\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n-1)-6\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 4.17.2 – 4.17.4.</para><para>The correct answer is \(k(1)=12, k(n)=k(n-1)-6\). </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
